.TH VM 3
.SH NAME
vm \- virtual machines (hypervisor)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.EX
bind -a #H /dev

/dev/vm/clone
/dev/vm/backend
/dev/vm/host
/dev/vm/\fIn\fP/ctl
/dev/vm/\fIn\fP/status
/dev/vm/\fIn\fP/mem
/dev/vm/\fIn\fP/console
/dev/vm/\fIn\fP/vcpu\fIm\fP/ctl
/dev/vm/\fIn\fP/vcpu\fIm\fP/regs
/dev/vm/\fIn\fP/vcpu\fIm\fP/exit
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Vm
runs other operating systems as guests of the native kernel, using
the processor's virtualization extension: Intel VMX with EPT and
unrestricted guest, or AMD SVM with nested paging, whichever the
processor has (on RISC-V, the hypervisor extension).
.B backend
says which, what it offers, and why not when there is none.
.B host
reports what the host has left for machines: processors, free memory
and the largest contiguous run of it (a machine's memory is one run),
and the machines and vCPUs running; it is what
.IR vmsched (1)
reads to place machines.
.PP
Opening
.B clone
makes a machine; the channel becomes its
.BR ctl ,
and reading it gives the machine's number
.IR n .
Writes to
.B ctl
are commands:
.TP
.BI mem " bytes"
Give the machine
.I bytes
of RAM (rounded up to 2 MiB) at guest-physical address 0.
.TP
.BI "io ignore" "\fR, \fP" "io exit"
What happens to a port nothing in the kernel claims: with
.BR ignore ,
a read gets all ones and a write is dropped; with
.B exit
(the default) the vCPU stops and the access appears in
.BR exit .
.TP
.BI assign " 0000:bb:dd.f"
Give the machine a PCI function (see BUGS for which), on its PCI bus:
on a PC reached through configuration mechanism 1; on RISC-V, where
the processor gives guests AIA, through an ECAM window at
0x30000000 with BARs from 0x40000000, its MSI-X sent into the guest's
IMSIC interrupt files.  Its memory BARs are mapped into the guest
uncached, except the pages of its MSI-X table, which the kernel keeps;
an entry the guest enables gets a host vector of its own whose
interrupt is delivered as the guest's message.  Its DMA goes through
an
.IR iommu (3)
domain mapping guest-physical addresses onto the machine's memory, so
the guest programs it with guest-physical addresses and it reaches
nothing else.  A function taken out of the IOMMU's blocking domain
(below) is taken from there.
.TP
.BI "virtio blk" " file"
Give the machine a virtio disk (virtio 1.0, PCI transport) served by
the kernel from
.IR file ,
resolved in the writer's namespace: read-write if it opens so,
read-only otherwise; its size is the file's, in 512-byte sectors.
.TP
.BI "virtio net" " etherdir"
Give the machine a virtio network interface on a new connection of the
.IR ether (3)
device
.I etherdir
opened as a bridge port (every type, promiscuous, bridge mode): the
guest's frames go out with its own address, which the device learns,
and frames for that address, broadcasts and multicasts come in.
The address is 52:49:\fIvm\fP:\fIdev\fP:00:01.
.TP
.BI vcpus " n"
Give the machine
.I n
processors (up to 8): vCPUs 1 to
.IR n \-1,
each a kernel process of its own on a processor of its own where the
host has enough, which wait, as application processors do, for the
INIT and SIPI the guest's bootstrap processor sends through its APIC.
The platform delivers inter-processor interrupts (fixed,
lowest-priority, NMI, INIT, SIPI) and MSIs to the APICs they name; a
vCPU in guest mode on another processor is taken out to see one by a
host IPI.
.TP
.BI traps " how"
Who answers the vCPUs' exits, before the machine first runs:
.B platform
(the default) the emulated machine below, making it a virtual
machine for a guest kernel;
.B none
nothing in the kernel, every exit going to
.BR exit ;
.B sys
this kernel's system calls, making it a process with hardware: its
own address space, rings and exceptions on the processor's
virtualization extension, its system calls Inferno's.  It runs in the
writer's namespace with a copy of the writer's file descriptors,
shared by its vCPUs.  A hypercall (VMCALL, or SVM's VMMCALL) is a
system call: the number in
.BR rax ,
the arguments in
.BR rdi ,
.BR rsi ,
.BR rdx ,
.B r10
and
.BR r8 ,
the result in
.B rax
(\-1 for an error);
buffers are guest-virtual addresses.  The calls are 0
.IR exits ( status ),
1
.IR open ( path , mode ),
2
.IR close ( fd ),
3
.IR read ( fd , buf , n ),
4
.IR write ( fd , buf , n ),
5
.IR pread ( fd , buf , n , off ),
6
.IR pwrite ( fd , buf , n , off ),
7
.IR seek ( fd , off , type )
and 8
.IR errstr ( buf , n ).
.I Exits
stops the vCPU with an
.B exited
exit carrying its status.
.TP
.BI trace " seconds"
While a vCPU runs, print on the host's console, at most every
.I seconds
(0 turns it off), its guest RIP, the reason of its last exit and its
count of exits: a heartbeat for telling a guest that is busy from a
host that has stopped.  While any machine is traced, a vCPU that is
running yet has taken no exit for three such periods has the host's
processes, and where each sleeps, printed once.
.TP
.B kill
Stop the machine and free its memory.  Before the memory goes, each
assigned function is released: its MSI-X vectors and bus mastering
off, moved straight from the machine's IOMMU domain into the blocking
domain of
.IR iommu (3),
where any DMA it still makes reaches nothing, and reset \(em by
function-level reset where it has one, else by a secondary bus reset
where it is alone below its bridge \(em with the host's configuration
of it put back.  The host's console says
.IP
.EX
vm0.PCI.5.0.0: released: blocked, reset by FLR
.EE
.IP
It stays blocked until assigned again or claimed through
.IR iommu (3).
Open files then return
.BR Ehungup ;
the structures go with the last close.
.PP
.B mem
reads and writes guest memory at offset = guest-physical address.
.B console
carries the guest's 16550 at
.BR 0x3F8 :
reads return what it wrote, writes are what it receives.
.B status
gives the machine, each vCPU's state, its exits counted by kind and
how many the kernel handled, the platform's counters, and each
assigned and virtio function with its interrupts.
.PP
Each vCPU is a kernel process wired to one processor, and does all
work on the processor's virtualization state itself.  Its
.B ctl
takes
.B go
(run until an exit the kernel does not handle),
.B skip
(advance past the exiting instruction, then go) and
.B stop .
.B regs
reads and writes
.RI \&" name " " value \&"
lines (general registers,
.BR rip ,
.BR rflags ,
control registers,
.BR efer ,
segment selectors, bases, limits and access rights in VMX format,
descriptor tables) while the vCPU is stopped.
.B exit
blocks until the vCPU stops and says why: the kind
.RB ( io ,
.BR mmio ,
.BR cpuid ,
.BR rdmsr ,
.BR wrmsr ,
.BR hlt ,
.BR vmcall ,
.BR exception ,
.BR triple ,
.BR entryfail ,
.BR stopped ,
.BR shutdown ,
.BR exited ,
.BR other ),
the guest's
.BR rip ,
the instruction length, the backend's own code and qualification, and
the details of the kind.
.SS The platform
What a guest sees is handled in the kernel, on the vCPU's processor:
.IP \(bu 2
an 8259 pair and an 8254 on IRQ 0, the 16550 on IRQ 4, and an I/O APIC
at 0xFEC00000 (id 8) with the PIT on its input 2 and the UART on 4;
.IP \(bu 2
a local APIC per vCPU (xAPIC MMIO at 0xFEE00000, or x2APIC through its
MSRs if the guest turns it on) whose timer runs off the TSC in
one-shot, periodic or TSC-deadline mode;
.IP \(bu 2
CPUID: the host's, with what the hypervisor does not provide hidden
(VMX and SVM, MONITOR/MWAIT, XSAVE and AVX, x2APIC, MTRR, machine
check, performance monitoring, INVPCID, RDTSCP, RDPID, LA57, PKU) and
the hypervisor bit set; leaf 0x40000000 says
.LR InfernoHyper ;
.IP \(bu 2
a paravirtual clock: KVM's kvmclock, offered at CPUID 0x40000100 with
KVM's signature and only its CLOCKSOURCE2 and stable-TSC features.
Writing an address to MSR_KVM_SYSTEM_TIME (0x4B564D01, or the old
0x12) gets that vCPU's record written there \(em the TSC, nanoseconds
since the machine was made, and the scale between them; the guest runs
on the host's TSC unoffset, so one record stays good \(em and
MSR_KVM_WALL_CLOCK (0x4B564D00, or 0x11) gets the host's time of day
at the machine's time 0.  That is the host's
.IR cons (3)
.BR time ,
which on a native host counts from boot until init sets it from
.IR rtc (3);
.IP \(bu 2
the MSRs: the processor's own switched with the guest, the APIC's,
the TSC, the TSC deadline and a few firmware would answer; others
raise #GP;
.IP \(bu 2
HLT (the vCPU sleeps until an interrupt it can take);
.IP \(bu 2
a PCI bus: assigned functions and the kernel's own virtio functions.
.PP
There is no RTC, HPET, or firmware; ACPI and the MP table, where a
guest needs them, are written into its memory by the program that
loads it
.RI ( vmlinux (1)
does), and Linux is told the TSC rate with
.BR tsc_early_khz= .
The guest's exceptions are its own: neither backend intercepts them
(an alignment check at CPL 3 is the guest's #AC), unless the
.B *vmxexcbitmap
of
.IR plan9.ini (10)
names some, each then an
.B exception
exit.
.SH EXAMPLE
Make a machine, give it memory, load and start it, and read why it
stopped:
.IP
.EX
bind -a '#H' /dev
{
	n=`{read}
	echo mem 268435456 >[1=0]
	echo assign 0000:05:00.0 >[1=0]
	# ... write the kernel into /dev/vm/$n/mem, set regs ...
	echo go > /dev/vm/$n/vcpu0/ctl
	cat /dev/vm/$n/vcpu0/exit
} <> /dev/vm/clone
.EE
.PP
.IR vmlinux (1)
does all of this, using nothing but these files, locally or over a
Styx mount of another host's
.BR /dev .
